<!-- wp:mbn-theme/section-location-heading-list-image {"backgroundColor":"#e5eef9","heading":"How an Attorney Can Help You With a Personal Injury Case","introParagraphs":["Navigating a personal injury case can be complex and challenging. An experienced attorney can provide invaluable assistance in various ways, ensuring that your rights are protected, and you receive the compensation you deserve. Here’s how:"],"listItems":[{"title":"Thorough Investigation","description":"An attorney will conduct a comprehensive investigation to gather crucial evidence, interview witnesses, and establish the facts needed to support your claim.","label":"","url":"","id":"mr0h5wfy3348x8bnc4i"},{"title":"Negotiation Skills","description":"Skilled attorneys are adept at negotiating with insurance companies and opposing counsel to reach favorable settlements without the need for a lengthy court process.","label":"","url":"","id":"mr0h5wfy5mtebaz3598"},{"title":"Representation in Court","description":"If a settlement cannot be reached, having an attorney by your side to represent you in court can be vital. They will present your case effectively, advocating on your behalf.","label":"","url":"","id":"mr0h5wfy17tcn3u9u52"}],"imageUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/img-location-surprise-how-atty-help-personal-injury-case.jpg","imageId":10907,"footerParagraphs":["By working with a personal injury attorney, you can focus on your recovery while they handle the legal intricacies of your case. "]} /-->

<!-- wp:mbn-theme/section-location-heading-list-image {"heading":"Compensation Available in Personal Injury Accident Cases","introParagraphs":["In the unfortunate event of a personal injury accident, victims may be entitled to various forms of compensation to help alleviate the financial burden and impact of their injuries. Here are some of the primary types of compensation available:"],"listItems":[{"title":"Medical Expenses","description":"Coverage for medical bills incurred as a direct result of the accident, including emergency room visits, hospital stays, surgeries, medications, and rehabilitation services.","label":"","url":"","id":"mr0hctoskx1qdxqhzg"},{"title":"Lost Wages","description":"Compensation for income lost due to time away from work while recovering from injuries. This can also include future lost earnings if the injury leads to long-term disability.","label":"","url":"","id":"mr0hctoszh5diax39j"},{"title":"Property Damage","description":"Reimbursement for any personal property damaged during the accident, such as clothing, jewelry, or electronic devices.","label":"","url":"","id":"mr0hctosxo05xehssl"},{"id":"mr0he7q0bd6dl6c98oh","title":"Pain and Suffering","description":"Monetary award for the physical pain and emotional distress experienced as a result of the accident. This is more subjective and can vary significantly based on individual circumstances.","label":"","url":""},{"id":"mr0hek7jbdedmh3feno","title":"Loss of Consortium","description":"Compensation provided to the family members of the victim, recognizing the impact the injury has on the victim’s relationships and family life.","label":"","url":""},{"id":"mr0heufacj4wamgvth","title":"Punitive Damages","description":"In cases where the accident was caused by particularly egregious or reckless behavior, <a href="https://www.justice.gov/d9/elderjustice/legacy/2015/12/15/Arizona_VHS_Acquisition_Corp_Brief.pdf" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.justice.gov/d9/elderjustice/legacy/2015/12/15/Arizona_VHS_Acquisition_Corp_Brief.pdf">punitive damages</a> may be awarded to punish the wrongdoer and deter similar actions in the future. Specifically, for punitive damages to be granted, there needs to be evidence of an “evil mind” or malicious intent behind the negligent actions.","label":"","url":""}],"imageUrl":"http://hastingsandhastings.dev.local/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/img-location-ahwatukee-comprehensive-accident.jpg","imageId":10808,"footerParagraphs":["A. Punitive Damages Standard To recover punitive damages at trial, Plaintiffs have the burden of proving by clear and convincing evidence that Defendants acted with an “evil mind.” Rawlings v. Apodaca, 151 Ariz. 149, 726 P.2d 565 (1986). The required state of mind may be shown by any of the following: 1. Evidence of conscious disregard for the health and safety of the tort victim; 2. Evidence of financial motive of the defendant motivating sub-standard conduct; or 3. Evidence of dishonesty, concealment of facts, fraud, or misleading statements to cover culpability."]} /-->

